Date: Tue, 23 Mar 93 15:28:11 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON/CRYPT: Crypto-Extortion

An influential friend whom I respect is seriously concerned that the
possibility of cryptographic support for extortion should make us
seriously question the desirability of wider use of cryptography.  So
I did the following analysis to see if we can find good enough ways to
discourage such extortion.

The basic scenario is that everyone *says* up front that they will not
give into extortion, but this claim may not be credible.  So an
extortionist sends an anonymous threat to someone, asking that a
certain amount of untraceable digital money be sent to them by a
certain date, demonstrating in some way that the extortionists knows
where they live or can be harmed, and claiming that if they are not
paid something very bad will happen to this victim.

The extortionist also makes reference to an extortionist rating
agency, who offers information relevant for estimating the chance that
this extortionist will follow through on a threat, and on the
additional number of similar threats the victim may expect if they
give in to this one.  If the victim's normal mechanisms for preventing
him/her from giving in have failed for some reason, the victim then
may give in if the ransom asked is less than the expected harm if
he/she doesn't give in.

More formally, let

H = the harm that a extortionst might do to you
R = the randsom amount asked
D = the cost to of of trying to defend against an attempt at harm

M = the harm that you and/or the law could do to you if you caught them
C = the cost of them trying to harm you

r = the probability you actually recieve and read their threat
e = the probability you assign that this extortionist is serious
g = the probability that you can give in, that the mechanisms you set
    up to prevent yourself from doing so fail
b = the probability the extortionist guesses that it is in your
    interest to give in, given the threat
h = the probability that an attempt to harm you succeeds

s = the probability that extortionist caught from message sent
t = the probability that extortionist caught trying to collect money
q = the probability that extortionist caught trying to harm victim

n = the increase in the number of similar extortion attempts in the 
    "next round" caused by giving in to this one.
m = the decrease in the number of similar extortion attempts in the 
    "next round" caused by not giving in to this one.

If we define x = rgb, the probability that you give in, then the
extortionist wants to threaten you if:

       1-x            s
  R >  ---(C + qM) + (- + t)M
        x             x 

(This ignores potential earnings from future extortions.)
Cryptography and related technologies threaten to dramatically reduce
s and t, eliminating the second term from the formula.

If we assume borderline choices about whether to give in, and similar
choices for induced future extortion attempts, then the victim wants
to give in if:

               1-n          n+l
  R < (D + ehH)---  -  (1-r)---(eH(h0-h) - D)
               1+m          1+m

where h0 is the chance of harm given D=0, and this second term comes
from the fact that you can't optimally defend if you never heard the
threat (and hence the poor defense term (eH(h0-h) - D) is positive).

With s=t=0, and ignoring the poor defense term, we find extortion is
not a viable business unless

 C +  qM     rgb  (1-n)
 ------- < ------------
 D + ehH   (1-rgb)(1+m)

Thus to discourage extortion, we want to lower D,e,h,H,r,g,b and want
to raise C,q,M,n,m.  Let's see how this might be done.

The technology of offense and defense for actual attempts at harm
determines how h is a functions of C and D.  All else equal,
easier extortion will induce more effort at defense, like security
buildings, body guards, etc.

The technology of crime detection determines how g is a function of C
and D.  Perhaps more hidden cameras and paid informants would be used.

The technology of commitment determines how g is a function of R.
Larger payments are harder to hide from one's family and associates,
who can help one enforcer prior commitments to not give in.  Larger R
also increases n,m because others are more likley to learn of your
having given in.  Ensuring that digital cash transfers above a certain
amount are known to associates provides a way to commit to not give in.

The technology of filtering determines r.  If one could send all
unsolicited messages to an extortion filter, which promises to filter
out all such messages from your view, r might be made low.  Or such
messages might always be forwarded to an associate in order to reduce g.

The technology of reputations determine e and n,m.  Some trusted rating
organization must collect records on who was threatened by whom,
whether they gave in, whether they were harmed, and how the rate of
threats to them changed after that point.  Extortionists could insure
that such an organization was knew a lot about each threat by cc-ing
them a copy of the threat, by requiring payment be cc-ed to them, and
by sending them info about where that person lives, etc.

However, it may be more difficult for extortionists to credibly
demonstrate that folks who have given in to them before have not
suffered many more credible threats after that as a result, or that
those who did not give in did not suffer many fewer credible threats.
Any one rating organization could not be expected to learn of all
extortion threats.

And even if they could demonstrate such a fact if true, it's hard to
see how they could insure it's truth, preventing other extortionists
from jumping on an apparently profitable victim.  

Without a plausible scenario for how such reputations could be created
and communicated, extortion seems less of a problem; potential victims
would estimate n,m so high that they would not think it in their
interest to give in, even if able.  This is hopeful, but before
breathing too easy we should work harder searching for such scenarios.

Robin Hanson

