Date: Tue, 23 Mar 93 18:24:13 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Extropianism as a Cargo Cult

[This message just arrived at my machine--must've been in some kind of
cryptographic time warp for the past week.]

Dale Worley, commenting on a remark by Ray Cromwell:

>     There are numerous extropians on this list involved with
>   Alcor, implementing cryptographic networks/cash, hypertext/distributed
>   object-oriented computer markets, information markets, refinements of
>   ppl criminology, and a lot more. A lot of these people are getting paid
>   to do this, what could be more practical?
>
>Too bad they don't write more here on what they're doing.

Actually, much of what gets written about *does* reflect our professsional
interests. For example, I've written extensively about crypto, digital
cash, etc., as have Marc Ringuette, Hal Finney, Duncan Frissell, Perry
Metzger, Sandy Sandfort and others. (I cofounded the "Cypherpunks" list,
where most of this discussion has moved.) Some helped out on the PGP
program, others are writing papers and articles. Check out upcoming issues
of "Wired," "Mondo 2000," "Whole Earth Review," and "The Village Voice" for
articles on this.) I'm impressed that such a large fraction of the active
posters are deeply involved in such a relevant technology.

The same applies to other areas. Owen Rowley, on this list until recently,
does VR research at Autodesk. Dean Tribble is a principal member of the
Xanadu hypertext team. Robin Hanson is developing betting systems for idea
futures. Brad Cox is the guy behind "Objective C," "software ICs," and
other object-oriented systems. David Friedman wrote "The Machinery of
Freedom," an oft-quoted framework for anarchocapitalist ideas.

A bunch of people are working on AI--perhaps a dozen on this list. Genetic
programming, news filters, etc.

And so on. As I've said before, pretty impressive.

People don't necessarily *write* about what they're doing for a simple
reason: they probably have *already* written about it, early in their
posting career.

Newcomers can always ask the oldtimers to repost their relevant essays.

-Tim May



