Date: Wed, 24 Mar 93 6:26:25 PST
From: more@chaph.usc.edu (Max More)
Subject: PHIL: Identity, Transformation, Uploading (LONG)

Re:  Identity, Transformation, and Transplanted Ant Brains
Max More, March 24 1993
. 
This is a rather delayed response to several postings on identity and
transformation between Feb 24 and 27.  My apologies to those bothered by
the length; I'm posting it here because in citing other posters it would
contravene the rules for ExI-Essay.
. 
Hal Finney compared the relation of our current selves to our selves of the
distant future to the results of implanting an ant's brain in a human brain:
. 
>Imagine somehow engineering an ant to become a human: perhaps transplanting
>its minuscule bit of neural tissue into the middle of a human brain,
>then teaching that human just as you would a baby until it grows into a
>productive member of society.  Is it still the same identity as the ant?
> I would say not.
>
>Yet it's possible (even likely, I'd say) that our future selves will be
>as dissimilar to us as we are to the ant.
. 
I think this analogy is inappropriate, unless Hal is assuming that in our
future lies some highly disruptive and discontinuous change that destroys,
rather than transforms or augments, most of what we were.  (Below, I'll
consider whether uploading would be like that.)  What happens to the ant?
I can imagine that somehow its memories are retained (whatever an ant's
memries are like subjectively), but practically everything else would go.
Asking if the baby with implant is the "same identity" as the ant can be
misleading, since "identity" can mean logical identity (if you-now are
numerically identical to you-yesterday then you are logically identical), or
qualitative identity (two temporally or spatially distinct things may be
qualitatively identical whether or not they are logically identical).  This
ambiguity, as well as the word's favoring a static conception of self, are
why I prefer to talk of personal continuity, or survival.  Where there is
continuity, there is logical - but not necessarily qualitative - identity.
. 
I agree that the ant does not survive the procedure.  That is, not enough of
the ant continues in the baby for it to count as logically the same thing.  I'm
no entomologist, but I doubt that an ant has much in the way of memory
(at least of a kind like ours), and so memory would be a minor part of its
constitution.  More important would be the ant's urges, desires, and
dispositions, which are shaped largely by its DNA and mediated by
chemical signals special to ants.  When implanted into the baby's brain, all
that would be lost along with the ant's biochemistry, leaving maybe some
memories of scuttering around doing the queen's bidding.  If you were to
make massive alterations in the baby's biochemistry and genetic code to
make it far more antlike (rather like in The Fly), and not much like a
human baby, then much more of the ant would survive, and I might say
the ant *had* survived.  Hal's example doesn't seem to be like that.
. 
The relation between my current self and my far future self is not like the
ant-implant, even if we stipulate that the magnitude of difference involved
is comparable.  The transition from ant to antbaby was both discontinuous
and heavily destructive to the ant.  But the transition from Max to PostMax
will take place over many years, in a gradual and continuous manner.  At
no point during that process do I expect a change as drastic and sudden as
in the ant case, certainly no change drastic enough to say that not enough
had continued for me to survive.  Furthermore, whereas the change
undergone by the ant destroyed most of what it was, I expect my changes
be transformative rater than disruptive, augmentative rather than
destructive.  (More on this below.)
. 
Hal said:  "But growth and death are two sides of the same coin.  By
committing to growth we agree to the death of the person we are today, in
the hopes that we will be a better person tomorrow."  Taken
metaphorically, this might be acceptable, but taken literally this way of
presenting the situation is not only incorrect (as I intend to show here) but
anti-extropic in the motivation that such a belief could generate.  I agree
with Mike Price's response when he stated that growth and death are
alternatives.  A view of identity or personal continuity promoting the belief
that change brings death clearly will not foster extropian commitment to
self-transformation and personal growth!  Of course, an extropian with this
view might choose self-sacrifice, self-destruction in favor of their superior
successor.  Still, such a self-conception seems open to abuse.  Fortunately,
not only is it anti-extropic, it is false.
. 
An alternative conception of personal identity, such as the one I've been
thinking about for my academic work, is more dynamic and
transformation-oriented, allowing change, development, and growth to
occur without bringing death.  To quote Mark Miller, whose independent
ruminations on the topic are parallel to mine:
. 
"So what I'm looking for is some notion of identity which has the right kind
of fluidity to it and still the right sense of coherence and continuity over
time and over change, such that, by using that notion of identity, it can be
an aid to intuition about post-Singularity life, not a hindrance to it."
[EXTROPY #10, p.27]
. 
Crucial to such a theory of personal continuity will be (a) a set of clear
distinctions between different types of change; and (b) an identity relation
which doesn't interpret all changes in self as partial death of that self.  On
the second of these, my thinking owes much to philosopher Derek Parfit, most 
of whose writing on the subject is in his extremely stimulating book
"Reasons and Persons" (Oxford University Press, 1984).  Philosophers
haven't thought much about (a), especially with regard to technological
changes, so I'm starting from the beginning.
. 
This dynamic conception of personal continuity is founded on Parfit's
account of psychological reductionism, a view I've explained here before,
way back in the early days of the list when we discussed identity.  Since
the proximate cause of my posting this is to respond to Hal, I'll explain
psychological reductionism briefly and focus on ideas concerning change
and continuity.  A reductionist theory of identity is one claiming that
questions about personal identity ("is this the same person?") can be
reduced to other facts.  A physical reductionist believes that those other
facts are bodily facts, i.e., person B at a later time is the same person as A
at earlier time if A and B have the same, persisting body (or brain, or enough
of the brain).  A psychological reductionist (any uploader is in this
category) thinks that what really matters in your survival is not the
continuity of your body (not even your brain), except as a practical matter,
but psychological continuity: the continuation of the intentions, desires, 
dispositions, and memories, that make you who you are.  If your psychology 
can continue despite the destruction of your body (as in uploading or Star Trek
style beaming), then you have continued.
. 
I will ignore the various flavors of psychological reductionism.  They all 
agree that surviving as logically the same person requires the continuation of 
your psychological characteristics.  In the EXTROPY interview, Mark Miller, 
made a suggestive analogy between survival of the Greek civilization and 
survival of persons.  Years ago, talking to his pediatrician, Mark expressed 
sorrow that the Greek civilization had died.  The response was that the Greek 
civilization had not died; we are it.  So many of the values of our 
civilization evolved from those of the Greek civilization that there is a sense
in which we are them.  Though I'm not sure about this case, it does capture the
essence of psychological reductionism.
. 
This model of identity distinguishes psychological continuity from 
psychologicalconnectedness.  Psychological connectedness is the holding of 
particular direct psychological connections, such as memories, intentions, 
dispositions.  Psychological continuity is the holding of overlapping chains of
strong connectedness.  Connectedness can hold to any degree.  If only one 
connection existed between X and Y, then X would not survive, would not 
continue in Y.  Strong connectedness exists where *enough* direct connections 
persist over a day.  Where we draw the line and declare there
to be "enough" connections, and thus that identity exists,
clearly is not precisely determined.  We can set a plausible boundary, say
where we require the persistence over the course of a day of 50% of the
number of connections that normally persist over that period.  We must a
draw the line because the logic of identity requires one, but we should
remember that what really matters is the underlying *degree* of
connectedness.  Once the criterion for strong connectedness is decided, we
can determine whether there has been an unbroken chain of *strong
connectedness*, i.e., psychological continuity.
. 
So, change doesn't mean death, so long as the changes we undergo at anytime 
are not so drastic as to destroy continuity.  I expect to undergo
multitudinous changes in my capacities, knowledge, and personality over
the coming decades and centuries, so that PostMax will have very few
direct connectios with me-now.  Yet Max and PostMax will be the same
person - I will have continued in PostMax - so long as I am not subjected to
forced and drastic neural restructuring, resulting in a wrenching
personality alteration into someone alien to my pre-operation values.
. 
My view is that what matters is continuity, not direct connectedness.  I look
forward to the prospect of PostMax being very different from me-now as
exciting and inspiring, not as a fearful prospect of doom.  To me, it isn't
crucial that PostMax have *any* of my current memories, though I prefer
that partly for purely historical interest, and partly because it is easier to
understand yourself and learn to improve if you can recall your past.  I
expect PostMax to want to retain my present (significant) memories, or at
least objectified records of them (my articles, diary entries, letters, email
postings, lists of books read...), but I regard memory as a relatively
unimportant component of myself.  (John Locke, a psychological
reductionist of centuries past, thought memory connections to be the only
connection that matters.)
. 
In order to sharpen our understanding of connectedness, Dave Krieger came up 
with a formalization that he posted to this list long ago, and which appeared 
as a column in Cryonics magazine sometime in the last year.  Without repeating 
Dave's whole explanation, the upshot is this:
The degree of connectedness may be measured from two points of view, and will 
usually be different for each.  Assume A is the future stage of a person (or 
it may be a copy of the person) and B is the current stage (or the original), 
then Y(A,B) is a measure of B in terms of A, of how A-like is B, while Y(B,A) 
is a measure of A in terms of B, of how B-like is A.  The equation (not 
reproduced here) produces a range between zero and one.  (Dave also
constructed a diagram of identity space from the points yielded.)  In a case 
where, over time, the individual retains almost all of his or her earlier 
characteristics but acquires many new ones in addition, Y(B,A) = close to 1, 
while Y(A,B) approaches zero increasingly as A expands.
. 
My view can be expressed by the claim that, so long as there is continuity 
between B (Max) and A (PostMax), B survives even when both Y(A,B) and Y(B,A) 
approach zero.  With the accumulation of changes over time, my present person-
stage may have little in common with my later person-stage, PostMax: Y(A,B) 
tends to zero, and perhaps also Y(B,A) eventually drops below 0.5 and heads for
zero.  This doesn't matter so long as my current person-stage
plays a major role in bringing about my next stage, and that next stage brings 
about a further stage..., and that stage causes PostMax to be.  Or, in Mark 
Miller's terms, I will have surrvived so long as
PostMax can give much of the *credit* for the way he (she? they? it?) is.
["Credit" can be a value-neutral term, simply meaning that the nature of A
largely explains how B came about; B might credit A, even though B is
worse than A (in terms of a value both might hold); e.g., A has poor self-
discipline, this results in a failure to work on maintaining and improving
the ability to concentrate for long periods of time, so B ends up with
weakened powers of concentration.]
. 
My view, then, is that I continue or survive into the remote future so long
as my current person-stage and a future person-stage are stages of one
continuous, diachronic individual, an individual we might refer to as the
"continuant self" in order to differentiate it from the self (person-stage) who
exists at any time, with a particular set of psychological features.  The
continuant self consists of an unbroken chain of overlapping links of strong
connectedness.  (I hope it's clear that I meana *psychologically* unbroken
chain; a temporal gap - a period where there is no living stage of the
continuant person - does not break the chain, so long as the living person-
stage following the temporal gap is strongly connected to the last pre-gap
person.  Thus a period of biostasis does not break continuity in the sense I
intend.)
. 
This raises a further, interesting question:  If I survive so long as 
continuity is maintained, yet my future person-stage is directly connected to 
me only weakly or not at all, is it rational for me to not care about my remote
future self?  On most views of personal identity or continuity, certainly the
traditional ones, if I am the same individual as a future person, it
automatically follows that I should care about that person's interests and
well-being.  The standard view claims that all temporal stages of me are
equally me, so that rationally I should sacrifice a minor current value if it
will secure a greater value for my future self (discounted by an uncertainty
factor, which I will ignore, although in practice it usually will be high). On
the psychological continuity view however, this claim is obviously open to 
dispute:  Even if Max and PostMax are stages of the same continuant person, 
they are at best very weakly connected, especially if PostMax has not only
outgrown Max - Y(A,B) close to zero - but has also lost most of Max's memories,
changed values, goals, and projects - Y(B,A) close to zero.  It is not obvious
that Max is rationally required to care about PostMax any more than he should 
care about any other person with whom he is largely unconnected.  It seems 
rationally defensible for us to care about our future stages only to the
extent to which they are psychologically connected to our current selves.
. 
Personally, I care about my remote future self far more than in proportion to 
the degree of connectedness, perhaps because I am confident that my future self
will be far *better* than me-now.  If I believed the contrary, my willingness 
to take into account PostMax's interests would track the degree of 
connectedness between us.  Other people may care little for their distant, 
weakly connected future selves, even if they expect them to be better than they
now are.  Though I feel differently, currently no convincing argument 
demonstrating the irrationality of this pattern of concern suggests itself to 
me.  Should I value a future self because he is like me-now, or because he 
embodies what I-now value positively?  While I favor the second alternative, 
the former seems to be rationally tenable.  It makes sense to me to absorb 
costs in the present when this will produce greater benefits for my superior 
future self, even if we have little in common, because my future self is better
according to what I now value.  To value a 
future self's interest less simply because they are different seems odd to me 
considering that I will become him, but this may be a preference lying beyond 
the realm of rational determination (though I hope to discover otherwise).  My
preference is undoubtedly explained in part by my tendency to conceive of
myself as essentially the continuant individual, whereas others may
conceive of themselves primarily in terms of their current stage.  So our
metaphysical self-conception may determine the rationality of our pattern
of concern for the remote future, but our choice of self-conception seems to
be an ultimate, normatively irreducible choice.
. 
These alternatives are, of course, points on a spectrum.  At the conservative
end, we care about our future self in direct proportion to the degree of
psychological connectedness between us, even when the future self is
closer to our ideal than is our current self.  Let's assume there is 90%
connectedness; we would be willing to endure a pain (the simplest
example) now only if it will save our future self from a pain 110% or more
as bad as the current pain.  On the radical end, we care only that continuity
is maintained, caring no less about a future self, so long as the differences
between that and our current self do not detract from what we now value.
We would then be willing to endure a pain now to save the future self from
a pain equal to it.  At intervening points along the spectrum, we would be
willing to endure a pain now to save the future self from a pain between
100% and 110% as bad as the current pain.
. 
Everyone other than those holding the most radical position would
partially discount benefits to less than fully connected future selves, yet
almost everyone is willing to give up some of their current characteristics -
those they evaluate negatively - in order to become a better person
according to their own standards.  This means we are willing partially to
lose connectedness (as Hal would put it: We are willing to partly die) so
long as the characteristics removed are those we evaluate negatively or
indifferently.  For us to die, there must be a sudden reduction in
connectedness drastic enough to drop us below our requirement for continuity. 
Would this happen if we upload?  And would we want to upload regardless?
. 
I expect a gradual merging of brain and computer rather than a sudden,
total uploading, but for my present purpose I'll assume that uploading
involves just such a jump from brain to artificial neural network.  First, if
the device had capacities close to those of the brain - similar processing
speed, memory buffer, phenomenology, power - then connectedness has
clearly been almost completely maintained.  Since our continuity means
the continuity of our psychological characteristics, our personality,
exchanging a biological medium of support for self in favor of an
inorganic medium, is insignificant to the degree of connectedness.  Most
uploading scenarios, however, envision dramatic changes attending this
switch.  I will consider only a few of these changes in arguing that
uploading would not mean death of the former individual, but I believe
these are representative.  (My points will apply equally to changes
induced by neurochemical modification rather than uploading.)
. 
We expect uploading to preserve all our memories - perhaps even in a
more accessible form, with memory retrieval aided by knowbots scanning
hypermedia indexes.  The rate at which we can process data should
increase tremendously.  We should be able simultaneously to hold far
more elements in our awareness.  We expect to retain more of what we
learn, more rapidly expanding our base of knowledge.  With faster
processing and a larger memory buffer, we will be able to consider vastly
more hypothetical responses to a situation and so proceed more intelligently.
The acuity and range of our sensory awareness will expand dramatically,
enabling us to detect more of the EM spectrum, to peer into the depths of
space (through space telescope hookups), to discriminate nanoscale objects,
to detect and analyze extremely low concentrations of chemicals in our
environment, to perform spectral analysis of elemental composition, to hear
higher and lower frequencies, to notice infinitesimal changes in
gravitational or magnetic fields, and so on.
. 
Apart from these sensory and intellectal augmentations, we may modify
or excise drives and emotions embedded in human biology by evolution.
Thus we may abolish hunger and thirst, at least in their human forms, and
some will either expunge sexual desire and sensation, or put them under
full conscious control, or alter their triggers and their phenomenology.  We
may exclude or greatly modify analogs of hormonal and neurochemical
influences on human emotion, behavior, and motivation.  This could allow
us to enter states of enduring, undistractable concentration, instantly
abolish old compulsions and addictions or create adaptive new ones, spend
much of our time in creative free association or blissful ecstasy or
emotionless computation.  These are impressive differences from the
limited condition in which we lowly humans find ourselves.  Are they such
as to mean death of the former self resulting from severe loss of
psychological connectedness?
. 
Some of these changes augment already existing intellectual and sensory
abilities rather than replacing them.  Adding new senses or more effective
memory access does not take away any of what I was.  All of these
connections are preserved, though they constitute a smaller portion of my
self than before.  In terms of Dave's notation again, considering only
augmentative changes:  Y(B,A) = 1, though Y(A,B) = 0.2 (or any number
closer to zero than to one).  Considering only these sorts of changes,
uploading is clearly a method of growth and not an unusual way of dying.
. 
If uploading brings momentous changes which genuinely alter pre-existing
elements of a personality, such as the existence, orientation, and 
involuntariness of sexual desire, or hunger and thirst, or the strength,
persistence, and voluntariness of emotional and cognitive states, then
Y(B,A) will be substantially less than unity -- a portion of the former
human self will not continue on in the uploaded posthuman.  However,
this reduction in connectedness seems to me definitely insufficient to
destroy continuity; the person would have been transformed but not
destroyed in the process.  Many connections to the old self remain, such
as all the memories, most of the abilities and capabilities (including
physical abilities if a suitable body is inhabited), and the larger portion of
the person's beliefs, desires, intentions, dispositions, and personal
projects.
. 
Further, the true underlying degree of reduction in connectedness can be
less than it appears at first glance.  Certainly a large change in behavior is
compatible with a minor difference in personality:  Someone might
suddenly switch from being regularly deceitful to being characteristically
honest.  If, instead of these behaviors being expressions of fundamental
values of deceit and honesty, they are merely means to attaining a highly
valued end (such as "being successful"), the real underlying change in
personality will be slight.  A change of belief about the efficacy of deceit
vs. honesty led to the selection of different behavior in service to the very
same value.  In the uploading case too, some modifications in the
strength, objects, and expression of our desires and motivations may be
shaped by a higher-level feature of our personality.
. 
For example, sexual desire might be rewired to change its object from
certain sounds and sensations to efforts to solve difficult problems.  If we
value pleasure but spend more time solving problems than having sex, this
alteration in the desire makes it a better means of achieving the goal of
pleasure (and that of discovery!).  In the absence of a highly valued
intrinsic attachment to the *form* of sexuality, the reduction in
connectedness is smaller than initially apparent.  In general, if some
characteristics are more central to our character and so contribute heavily
to the measure of connectedness, then alterations or excisions of lesser
characteristics will reduce connectedness less than behavior alone might
suggest.  If our lives are directed and given form by fundamental values
and enduring projects and commitments, then the pruning and amendment
of desires to make them consistent should do little to reduce connectedness.
Uploading is desirable to us precisely because it helps promote values and
personal life projects; thus we should regard it continuation in superior
form and not as bringing about our death.
. 
Finally, on the idea of surviving in our children or by the dissemination of
our memes:  While certainly preferable to leaving behind no traces, they
are a very poor substitute for personal continuation.  Each of our ideas,
desires, beliefs, values, and intentions is interrelated with others in our
personality.  Part of their significance to us derives from their place in our
ecology of self.  Even if a belief or intention is taken up by another person,
it will have different relations to other psychological elements and so a
different significance.  Even if every one of my characteristics were to be
continued, but spread over a multitude of different persons, I would be
very far from surviving since I am more than an unordered collection of
parts.
. 
Although long for a posting to the list, this response is far from complete.
Since I will be developing these ideas into part of a dissertation chapter,
your critical comments would be appreciated.
. 
